This paper investigates the existence and nonexistence of generalized solutions to the nonlinear fractional Choquard equations given by

\begin{equation}
\left(-\Delta\right)^{s}\varphi+V\varphi=\varphi^{p}\varphi^{-q}\int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}\frac{|\varphi|^{l}dx}{|x|^{2s}}. \end{equation}
In this equation, $V$ is a positive function and the exponents $p$, $q$, and $l$ satisfy certain conditions. We consider this equation in a bounded region of $\mathbb{R}^{N}$ where $N\geq3$, $s\in(0,1)$, and $V$ is a positive function that can be expressed as a sum of two functions, one of which grows polynomially like $|x|^{a}$ as $|x|$ approaches infinity and the other decays like $e^{\lambda |x|^2}$ for some $\lambda>0$. We examine the existence of the ground state solution to this equation and the conditions under which the solution becomes trivial. These conditions involve the behavior of the function $V$ at infinity and the values of the exponents $p$, $q$, and $l$. In particular, we consider cases where $V$ grows polynomially like $|x|^{a}$ and cases where $V$ decays exponentially like $e^{\lambda |x|^{2}}$. We also consider cases where $p$, $q$, and $l$ are equal, and cases where $p$, $q$, and $l$ are distinct. We establish the existence of the ground state solution to this equation under various conditions on $V$, $p$, $q$, and $l$. Our results show that the ground state solution to this equation exists if and only if certain conditions are met. In particular, we show that the ground state solution to this equation exists if and only if $V$ grows polynomially like $|x|^{a}$ and $p$, $q$, and $l$ are equal. We also show that the ground state solution to this equation does not exist if and only if $V$ decays exponentially like $e^{\lambda |x|^{2}}$ and $p$, $q$, and $l$ are equal. In addition, we show that the ground state solution to this equation does
